    "available_at": "2026-10-13T09:05:04.974090+00:00",
    "body": "The Port of Los Angeles handled 1,042,652 twenty-foot equivalent units (TEUs) in September, making it the busiest month in the port’s 118-year history. Cargo volume rose 18.1% from the previous year, beating the previous monthly record set in July 2025 by more than 20,000 containers .\nWhat’s Related\nSeptember also capped the port’s busiest quarter, with approximately 3 million TEUs handled from July through September. The June-through-September stretch reached nearly 4 million TEUs, setting a separate four-month record.\nImports reached an all-time monthly high of 546,696 TEUs, up 19% from September 2025 and 26% above the port’s five-year average. Exports increased 10% to 125,571 TEUs, while empty containers rose 20.4% to a record 371,206 TEUs.\nThrough the first nine months of the year, the port handled 8,081,625 TEUs, up 3.38% from the same period in 2025. Executive Director Gene Seroka said those results put Los Angeles on a strong pace heading into the fourth quarter.\nRELATED: Top 30 U.S. Ports Ranked By Container Imports\nHoliday merchandise and inventory replenishments\nOn a media call, Seroka pointed to lower tariffs , continued consumer spending, and shipping disruptions as reasons for the increase. Major retailers and other importers are bringing in holiday merchandise while replenishing inventories.\n“A few things came together all at once,” said Seroka. “Tariffs are lower than the levels we saw earlier this year and last, giving importers more certainty on costs and allowing them to replenish inventories at a quicker rate. The American economy continues to move forward, and consumers are still buying.”\nSeptember marked the 10th time Los Angeles has handled more than 500,000 loaded import containers in a month. Seroka said no other U.S. port has reached that mark once.\nDespite the year-over-year increase in exports, Seroka said outbound cargo remained softer than usual. The record number of empty containers was expected, he added, as ocean carriers moved equipment back to Asia following the surge in imports.\nDisruptions bring additional cargo to Los Angeles\nContinued challenges along the Suez and Panama Canal routes are also sending more shipments through Los Angeles, Seroka said. He initially estimated those disruptions could increase the port’s cargo volume by about 5%, but now expects a larger gain.\nAsked by Logistics Management about the next three to six months, Seroka said another trade announcement or social media post from Washington could create renewed uncertainty. Still, he expressed confidence in the port’s outlook and pointed to continued economic growth as a reason for optimism.\n“But I think, in general, we look pretty good,” he said. “The American economy is still moving forward, and if we grow even at 1.5%-to 2% GDP growth, that's meaningful on a $32 trillion American economy, and it's extremely important to those trading partners we have around the world.”\nFor more on the port’s September results and Seroka’s outlook, read Jeff Berman’s full story in Logistics Management.",
